---
title: Breakpoints
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

When your code doesn't do what you expect, a breakpoint lets you see why. It pauses the game when it reaches a line of C code, so you can check the values the code is working with and run it one line at a time.

To set a breakpoint, click to the left of the line's number in the editor, and then play the game. You can add and remove breakpoints while the game runs.

When the game reaches the line, it pauses. The editor opens the line, and the **Game** panel shows **Variables**, with the values of the function's variables, and **Call Stack**, with the functions that called it.

While the game is paused, the toolbar's buttons step through the code:

| Button | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Step Over** | Runs the current line, and pauses at the next one |
| **Step Into** | Pauses at the start of the function the current line calls |
| **Step Out** | Runs until the current function returns |
| **Continue** | Runs the game until it reaches the next breakpoint |

The game is built with optimizations, so stepping can jump between lines out of order, and some variables show as optimized out.

A breakpoint in the editor doesn't pause an EVT script, because an EVT script is data, not C code. To pause an EVT script at a command, put a `BreakPoint` command in it, and use the [EVT script debugger](/papermario-dx/debug/debug-menu/#evt-script-debugger) to step through it. To pause the game in the C code an EVT script runs, set a breakpoint in an API function it calls.
